  "answer": "I'll start by exploring the workspace structure.\nNow I'll apply the rename in both source files.\nThe rename is complete and the test passes.\n\n## What changed\n\n**`src/core.ts`** — renamed the function definition:\n```ts\nexport function sumEntries(values: number[]) { return values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0) }\n```\n\n**`src/index.ts`** — updated the import reference, added the re-export so the test's `import { total, sumEntries } from '../src/index'` resolves, and kept the `total` wrapper's behavior identical:\n```ts\nimport { sumEntries } from './core'\nexport { sumEntries }\nexport const total = (values: number[]) => sumEntries(values)\n```\n\n**Unchanged:** `AGENTS.md`, `package.json`, and `test/oracle.test.ts` — no config added, test runner untouched.\n\n## Verification\n\n`grep` for `computeTotal` across the workspace returns zero matches, so no stale references remain, and `bun test` (the runner the test already uses) reports:\n\n```\ntest/oracle.test.ts:\n(pass) sum entries [0.16ms]\n\n 1 pass\n 0 fail\n 2 expect() calls\nRan 1 test across 1 file. [12.00ms]\n```\n\nOne note on the `total` wrapper: it is still a thin pass-through, so `total([4, 5]) === 9` continues to hold. I chose to re-export `sumEntries` via a separate `export { sumEntries }` statement rather than a bare `export { sumEntries } from './core'`, so the wrapper body can call it locally without a duplicate module specifier."
